### Three-Lane Delivery And Project Work-Level Policy

| Lane | Development / qualification side | Production / released side |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Web | Exact-commit image and generated site in `tsrookarocom-dev` | Exact released image and generated site in `tsrookarocom` |
| Windows installation | Disposable `gogetter.local` workspace `E:\\dev\\ts_windows_test_bed` | The same tagged Tool Shed snapshot installed into real Windows workspaces |
| Linux installation | Disposable `sup.local` workspace `/home/jon/dev/ts_linux_test_bed` | The same tagged Tool Shed snapshot installed into real Linux workspaces |

Git remains the single canonical code authority. A release record identifies one exact source SHA
and the lanes required by that change. Each required lane stores target identity, artifact digest
or image tag, qualification evidence, production/released-install evidence, and a terminal or open
disposition. Missing lineage or evidence fails open rather than being inferred complete.

The project-only work-level wrapper keeps standard cumulative behavior intact:

- Work3 `before` resolves and records the candidate's required lanes without claiming that the
  final frozen commit already exists.
- Standard Work3 runs unchanged against the configured split development environment.
- Work3 `after` binds the manifest to the resulting exact candidate commit, verifies and records
  every required development/test-bed lane, and refuses completion while any required lane remains
  open.
- This project's Work5 envelope suppresses only this selected endpoint's default execution with
  `run_default: false`. Its required replacement action preserves the standard Work5 sequence but
  interleaves lane preflight, production/install verification, evidence recording, and recursive
  reconciliation before any cohort or parent can finalize.
- Work5 then performs the existing GitHub-issue review. Failure of any required replacement or
  after-action leaves Work5 and its parents open.

The YAML action strings are routing policy, not a separate hook runtime. Deterministic project
tooling should implement lane resolution, development verification, release preflight, production
verification, and reconciliation so the policy is executable, fast, and auditable. The recursive
lineage and cached descendant-status mechanism belongs to `IDEA-0019`; this Idea consumes it for
release-lane closure rather than defining a second closure authority.

### Selected Initial Integration Policy

| Integration / side effect | Initial policy | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Maintainer login and MFA | Enable with a development-only account, password, TOTP seed, and session secret | Authentication and MFA are promotion gates, but plain HTTP means no production credential may be reused |
| Database migrations, retention, and dashboard views/SSE | Enable against the isolated development database | These are core application behaviors and do not contact an external system |
| Reporter enrollment, credential issuance, and report ingestion | Enable for dedicated development Linux and Windows project identities that connect only to the development server | Exercises the complete reporter communication contract without redirecting a production-connected workspace or copying a production credential |
| App Server and work-efficiency telemetry | Enable as bounded fields in reports from both development projects | The development dashboard receives realistic state while remaining advisory and unable to start App Server work or mutate a workspace |
| Reporter safety scheduler and persistent worker | After one manual enrollment and report handshake per project, install and run them as part of the initial development environment | Keeps continuous dev-server, dev-Linux, and dev-Windows communication active while using only development credentials and destinations |
| Email, webhooks, and hosted completion notifications | Keep disabled/absent | The current dashboard sends no email or webhooks; adding outbound delivery would create unrelated risk and scope |
| Production reporters, credentials, data, and scheduled jobs | Never connect or copy them into development | Preserves the approved isolation boundary and prevents misleading production state |

## Decisions

- A separate development site is needed before continuing routine public-site development.
- The public site is the promotion target, not the place for ordinary iteration.
- Development will run on the production server `sup` at `192.168.7.5` in a separately named and
  isolated Docker Compose project with its own host filesystem tree. Dev container and web-service
  restarts are in scope; host reboots and Docker-daemon restarts are not part of the expected
  development workflow.
- Production's current Docker environment is the `tsrookarocom` Compose project rooted at
  `/home/jon/docker/ts.rookaro.com`. Development will mirror its Nginx/docs, Django dashboard, and
  PostgreSQL service shape under a distinct Compose project and filesystem root.
- Development will remain LAN-only. It will not receive a public Nginx Proxy Manager or Rookaro
  router entry; production remains publicly available through its existing route without change.
- The Linux test bed on `sup` and Windows test bed on `gogetter` will access the development site
  over the LAN.
- Dedicated development Linux and Windows project identities will enroll with the development
  dashboard, receive development-only reporter credentials, submit reports and bounded telemetry,
  and run their normal reporter schedulers after an initial manual handshake. This continuous
  three-way development communication is part of the environment, not deferred functionality.
- Existing production-connected project identities, reporter credentials, queues, and schedulers
  will not be redirected or reused; the one-saved-dashboard-destination constraint is satisfied by
  the dedicated development project identities.
- Workpc will have persistent access through an automatically started, automatically restarted
  localhost-only SSH tunnel into `sup`; access must not depend on an active VS Code session.
- The development endpoint will use plain HTTP on port `8443`, with LAN clients using
  `http://192.168.7.5:8443` and Workpc using a localhost-only forwarded endpoint. Development will
  not use TLS/SSL, public DNS, Nginx Proxy Manager, or the public Rookaro hostname router.
- Development will begin with an empty database and deterministic synthetic seed data. Production
  credentials, reporter enrollments, sessions, and personal data will not be cloned.
- Development will carry unmistakable visual and machine-readable environment identity, including
  distinct UI banners, database names, Compose names, and environment markers.
- Initial deployment will be manual from a dedicated development branch or an exact frozen commit;
  automatic deployment on every push is deferred until the environment contract is proven.
- Promotion will use the exact commit tested in development, rather than rebuilding or selecting
  different source for production.
- The web, Windows-installation, and Linux-installation paths are three delivery lanes from one
  canonical repository, not six independently editable codebases. Test beds, generated deployment
  roots, images, and installed snapshots are derived and identify their source SHA.
- Every release candidate in this project requires all three lanes. Work3 and Work5 fail closed on
  any lane whose status is open, unknown, stale, missing lineage, or missing evidence; changing the
  required set is a project-policy revision, not a per-release bypass.
- This repository will leave portable Tool Shed Work1 through Work5 definitions unchanged. Work3
  extends its default with tracked project-only actions; Work5 uses a project-only replacement
  envelope because its production lane evidence must be recorded before final reconciliation.
- The previous project-only Work3 after-action that named the public deployment was incorrect for
  split mode and is replaced by exact-candidate development/test-bed qualification and evidence.
- Promotion requires evidence that the development stack deploys and becomes healthy; migrations
  and recovery work on non-production data; login, MFA, dashboard pages, and reporter enrollment
  work; both test beds connect; Workpc forwarding works; development is unreachable through public
  routing; and production remains healthy and unchanged.
- Development will use separate volumes, database, networks, secrets, logs, and backups, with CPU
  and memory limits. If the shared-host option is retained, those limits also protect production.
- Production and development will use distinct deployment/check-out roots on `sup`, distinct image
  tags, and no shared writable bind mounts. The exact filesystem paths will be chosen during
  implementation.
- Development must not reuse production's fixed `ts-rookaro-com` container name, production network,
  production volumes, `.env`, generated `public/` directory, or Nginx configuration.
- The operating contract will include development rollback and database-reset procedures. Before
  any eventual promotion involving migrations, production will be backed up.
- The initial integration/side-effect policy is selected as documented above. Email, webhooks, and
  hosted completion notifications remain absent because the current dashboard does not implement
  those senders; their absence does not restrict dashboard reporting between the development
  server and development projects.

## Don't Forget

- Give the development environment a persistent visual banner and machine-readable environment
  marker.
- Ensure filesystem separation includes deployment files, environment files, generated public
  assets, database storage, logs, and backup destinations—not only different Compose filenames.
- Use separate databases, volumes, secrets, enrollment credentials, cookies/session scope, and
  backup/restore paths.
- Prevent development reporters from being mistaken for production reporters and prevent existing
  production clients from silently reporting to development.
- Exercise database migrations and rollback/recovery using non-production data before promotion.
- Consider resource contention, monitoring, retention, backups, and cleanup—not just the
  application container.
- Update `work/tool-shed.yaml` from `combined` to `split` only after the development target and its
  operating contract are real and verified.
- Bind any Workpc-side forwarded port to `127.0.0.1`, not a public or all-interface address.
- Manage the Workpc tunnel with host-local startup and restart-on-failure behavior, SSH keepalives,
  and fail-fast port-binding checks; do not store a private key in the repository.
- Avoid a public `*.rookaro.com` development hostname because the existing wildcard DNS and TLS
  entry path could expose the environment accidentally.
- Configure development-specific allowed hosts, CSRF origins, cookie scope, and forwarded-protocol
  handling for plain-HTTP LAN access and Workpc localhost forwarding.
- Add a development-only Django security profile that permits plain HTTP without weakening the
  production HTTPS redirect, secure-cookie, proxy-header, or HSTS settings.
- Keep lane and environment identities explicit in commands, manifests, dashboard status, and
  evidence. Never let an unqualified target name or mutable working tree select promotion source.
- Ensure the project-only Work5 replacement preserves every standard Work5 obligation while
  interleaving lane-manifest preflight and production evidence before final reconciliation;
  lower-level custom envelopes are not replayed when a higher cumulative endpoint is selected.

